Q.1 .
Size curve = distribution of customer demand across different sizes.
Size grid = the set of sizes that a store/product/channel actually carries.
Size availability = whether the required size is actually available to the customer when they want to buy it.
Size productivity = how effectively each size contributes to sales/demand relative to the inventory or space allocated to it.

| #      | Term                       | Simple meaning                                                                                                       |
| ------ | -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **1**  | **Sell-through**           | % of inventory sold during a period. **Formula:** Units Sold ÷ Units Available × 100                                 |
| **2**  | **Stockout**               | When a product/size has **zero inventory available** despite customer demand.                                        |
| **3**  | **In-stock %**             | % of time or product-size combinations where inventory is available. Higher = better availability.                   |
| **4**  | **Weeks of Supply (WOS)**  | How many weeks the current inventory can support expected demand. **Inventory ÷ Weekly Demand**                      |
| **5**  | **Inventory Turnover**     | How quickly inventory is sold and replaced. Higher turnover generally means inventory is moving faster.              |
| **6**  | **Demand**                 | The quantity customers **want to buy**, whether or not inventory was available.                                      |
| **7**  | **Forecast**               | Estimated future demand/sales based on historical data, trends, seasonality, etc.                                    |
| **8**  | **Allocation**             | Deciding **how much inventory to send to each store/channel/location**.                                              |
| **9**  | **Replenishment**          | Restocking inventory when existing inventory gets low or demand requires more stock.                                 |
| **10** | **Size Curve**             | Distribution of demand/sales across different sizes. Example: S=20%, M=35%, L=30%, XL=15%.                           |
| **11** | **Size Availability**      | Whether the **size customers want is actually available** for purchase.                                              |
| **12** | **Full-price Sales**       | Sales made at the **original/list price**, without markdown or discount.                                             |
| **13** | **Markdown**               | Reducing the selling price of a product to stimulate sales or reduce excess inventory.                               |
| **14** | **Clearance**              | Selling remaining/excess/old inventory, usually at a **significantly reduced price**, to clear stock.                |
| **15** | **Pre-season Planning**    | Planning **before the selling season**—forecasting demand, deciding assortment, size grid, inventory and allocation. |
| **16** | **In-season Optimization** | Making **real-time adjustments during the season** based on actual sales, demand, inventory, stockouts and trends.   |


